{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UfUc45v146Fzw1FVXiUmDB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## CONTEXT\n2\t\n3\tThis prompt supports an English technical report for project `AR-S2J2J.R`. The audited project workflow uses EEMD-based decomposition and reconstruction of blast vibration acceleration histories. The research gap concerns how much can be claimed, which limitations must be stated, and which validation checks are expected when EEMD is used as an engineering filtering/reconstruction method.\n4\t\n5\t## SLOTS\n6\t\n7\t### SLOT 1: Authoritative EMD/EEMD Filtering References\n8\t\n9\tIdentify the EMD/EEMD filtering references that are most authoritative for engineering vibration or transient signal records. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n10\t\n11\t### SLOT 2: Supported Component Removal Practices\n12\t\n13\tAssess what the literature supports regarding removal of high-frequency IMFs, residual or trend terms, or selected modal components in EMD/EEMD filtering and reconstruction. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n14\t\n15\t### SLOT 3: Known Limitations To Report\n16\t\n17\tExplain the known limitations that should be reported, including mode mixing, end effects, non-uniqueness, sensitivity to ensemble or noise parameters, and loss of physical interpretability. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n18\t\n19\t### SLOT 4: Validation Checks For EEMD-Filtered Histories\n20\t\n21\tIdentify validation checks that are recommended or defensible for EEMD-filtered engineering histories, including peak preservation, energy preservation, frequency content, drift or baseline checks, response spectra, visual review, and any other relevant diagnostics. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n22\t\n23\t### SLOT 5: Description Without Overclaiming\n24\t\n25\tExplain how EEMD filtering should be described without overclaiming that retained modes are physically unique blast-wave components. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n26\t\n27\t## CONSTRAINTS\n28\t\n29\t- Prepare material for an English technical report.\n30\t- Focus on claim support, limitations, and validation expectations for EEMD as an engineering filtering/reconstruction method, not on basic definitions of EMD or EEMD.\n31\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9958eae5-5a95-4e8c-919f-b61a93167db8","uuid":"ded730f4-c32a-49ec-a044-9cbf2bea610b","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:43:56.482Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/md/gap-eemd-filtering-validation.prompt.md","content":"## CONTEXT\n\nThis prompt supports an English technical report for project `AR-S2J2J.R`. The audited project workflow uses EEMD-based decomposition and reconstruction of blast vibration acceleration histories. The research gap concerns how much can be claimed, which limitations must be stated, and which validation checks are expected when EEMD is used as an engineering filtering/reconstruction method.\n\n## SLOTS\n\n### SLOT 1: Authoritative EMD/EEMD Filtering References\n\nIdentify the EMD/EEMD filtering references that are most authoritative for engineering vibration or transient signal records. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 2: Supported Component Removal Practices\n\nAssess what the literature supports regarding removal of high-frequency IMFs, residual or trend terms, or selected modal components in EMD/EEMD filtering and reconstruction. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 3: Known Limitations To Report\n\nExplain the known limitations that should be reported, including mode mixing, end effects, non-uniqueness, sensitivity to ensemble or noise parameters, and loss of physical interpretability. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 4: Validation Checks For EEMD-Filtered Histories\n\nIdentify validation checks that are recommended or defensible for EEMD-filtered engineering histories, including peak preservation, energy preservation, frequency content, drift or baseline checks, response spectra, visual review, and any other relevant diagnostics. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n### SLOT 5: Description Without Overclaiming\n\nExplain how EEMD filtering should be described without overclaiming that retained modes are physically unique blast-wave components. Write in flowing paragraphs.\n\n## CONSTRAINTS\n\n- Prepare material for an English technical report.\n- Focus on claim support, limitations, and validation expectations for EEMD as an engineering filtering/reconstruction method, not on basic definitions of EMD or EEMD.\n","numLines":31,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31}}}
