{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WV3Dgsftzxf1boexqbbDt9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t### SLOT 1: Authoritative EMD/EEMD Filtering References\n2\t\n3\tThe most authoritative starting point is Huang et al. (1998), because it introduced empirical mode decomposition as the decomposition stage of the Hilbert-Huang transform for nonlinear and non-stationary records and established the intrinsic mode function and residual reconstruction framework used by later filtering studies. This source supports EMD as an adaptive decomposition of a measured record, but it does not by itself justify treating any retained intrinsic mode function as a uniquely physical engineering component. [@Ref001] The central EEMD reference is Wu and Huang (2009), because it defines ensemble empirical mode decomposition as a noise-assisted method in which finite-amplitude white noise is added over repeated trials and ensemble averaging is used to reduce mode mixing and obtain a scale-organized decomposition. [@Ref002] Flandrin, Rilling, and Goncalves (2004) and Wu and Huang (2004) are also authoritative for filtering claims because they connect EMD behavior to filter-bank-like and noise-scale behavior, which is the main basis for describing IMFs as adaptive scale bands rather than preassigned physical modes. [@Ref003][@Ref004]\n4\t\n5\tFor engineering filtering rather than basic decomposition, Boudraa and Cexus (2007) and Kopsinis and McLaughlin (2009) are more directly relevant because they treat EMD as a signal filtering or denoising tool and address component selection or thresholding in measurement and signal-processing contexts. [@Ref005][@Ref006] Huang and Wu (2008) is a useful review source because it summarizes Hilbert-Huang transform applications in geophysical records and discusses open problems, making it appropriate for limitation language in an engineering report. [@Ref007] For transient acceleration histories, the EMD/EEMD sources are best paired with strong-motion processing references such as Boore and Bommer (2005), Boore (2005), and Boore and Akkar (2003), because those papers document the consequences of filtering, baseline correction, and filter choices on accelerograms and response spectra. [@Ref008][@Ref009][@Ref010]\n6\t\n7\t### SLOT 2: Supported Component Removal Practices\n8\t\n9\tThe literature supports component removal only as a documented filtering or detrending operation followed by reconstruction, not as automatic evidence that the removed or retained IMFs are physically unique blast-wave components. EMD/EEMD decompositions are adaptive and reconstructive, so a processed history can be formed by summing selected IMFs and, when appropriate, a residual term; however, the selection criterion has to be stated as an engineering or signal-processing rule, such as removal of components dominated by high-frequency noise, removal of a low-frequency trend, or retention of components inside a target response band. [@Ref001][@Ref002][@Ref003] A defensible report therefore presents IMF removal as scale-selective filtering based on evidence from the record, not as a decomposition into uniquely named source mechanisms. [@Ref007]\n10\t\n11\tRemoval of early, high-frequency IMFs is supported when those IMFs are shown to be dominated by measurement noise or by frequencies outside the engineering response range of interest. Boudraa and Cexus support EMD-based signal filtering, and Kopsinis and McLaughlin support denoising approaches that treat IMF content with thresholding logic rather than a blanket rule that the first IMF is always noise. [@Ref005][@Ref006] Removal of a residual or trend term can be defensible when the residual represents baseline drift or nonphysical low-frequency contamination, but the same operation can also remove real long-period motion; in acceleration-history applications it therefore has to be treated as a high-pass or trend-removal choice whose consequences are checked in integrated velocity, displacement, and response spectra. [@Ref008][@Ref009][@Ref010]\n12\t\n13\tSelection of nonadjacent or middle IMFs is also supported only when the selection is tied to a stated diagnostic, such as frequency content, energy contribution, correlation with the target signal, response-spectral relevance, or comparison with independent instrumentation or physics. Rilling and Flandrin show that EMD separation of nearby frequency content depends on signal conditions, so component selection cannot be assumed to isolate every true modal contribution in a short transient record. [@Ref011] EEMD reduces mode mixing by adding noise-assisted ensemble averaging, but the method still depends on ensemble size, added-noise amplitude, sifting choices, and endpoint treatment, so retained IMF groups are better described as selected scale bands than as verified physical modes. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n14\t\n15\t### SLOT 3: Known Limitations To Report\n16\t\n17\tMode mixing is a primary limitation to report. EEMD was proposed specifically to reduce the tendency of EMD to place disparate scales in the same IMF or split a similar scale across several IMFs, but its remedy uses added white noise and ensemble averaging rather than a deterministic physical separation. [@Ref002] This means that the reported processing has to identify the added-noise level, ensemble size, sifting or stopping criterion, randomization policy when relevant, and any post-processing or alignment method used to compare IMFs across trials. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n18\t\n19\tEnd effects, non-uniqueness, and parameter sensitivity are also material limitations. EMD envelopes are built from local extrema, so behavior near the beginning and end of a short record can be sensitive to interpolation and boundary extension choices; this is especially important for transient vibration histories in which the main pulse, decay tail, or baseline segment may occur near a boundary. [@Ref001][@Ref007] The decomposition is not a fixed orthogonal transform with a predeclared basis, and different sifting, stopping, noise, and endpoint choices can change the IMF set; therefore, the same measured history can yield different but plausible component partitions. [@Ref003][@Ref002]\n20\t\n21\tLoss of physical interpretability is a limitation whenever IMFs are used for report-level engineering claims. EMD and EEMD components are derived from local time-scale behavior, so the existence of an IMF group does not by itself demonstrate a separate blast source, wave path, soil layer response, sensor artifact, or structural mode. [@Ref001][@Ref011] Filtering can also alter peak values, apparent pulse duration, low-frequency drift, and response demands; those consequences are well documented in strong-motion filtering and baseline processing literature and are relevant when processed acceleration histories are used downstream. [@Ref008][@Ref010]\n22\t\n23\t### SLOT 4: Validation Checks For EEMD-Filtered Histories\n24\t\n25\tValidation of an EEMD-filtered acceleration history is strongest when it compares the raw record, the retained reconstruction, and the removed residual content in both time and frequency domains. Peak preservation checks include the signed and absolute peak acceleration, peak timing, pulse polarity, and local pulse shape near the peak, because a filter that improves visual smoothness can still alter the controlling demand in a transient history. [@Ref008][@Ref009] Baseline and drift checks include numerical integration to velocity and displacement, comparison of terminal velocity and displacement trends, and review of any low-frequency residual removed from the reconstruction. [@Ref008]\n26\t\n27\tEnergy and frequency-content checks are defensible because EMD filtering is scale-selective. The report can compare root-mean-square acceleration, summed squared acceleration, cumulative signal energy, Fourier or power spectral density estimates, Hilbert marginal spectra when used, and the frequency content of removed IMFs to confirm that the operation removed the intended scale range rather than suppressing engineering demand. [@Ref005][@Ref006][@Ref004] Response-spectrum checks are important when the processed acceleration is used as an engineering input, because response spectra can be sensitive to filtering choices and the difference may be consequential even when the acceleration trace appears acceptable. [@Ref010][@Ref008]\n28\t\n29\tRepeatability and visual diagnostics are also necessary for EEMD-filtered histories. Because EEMD depends on added-noise amplitude and ensemble averaging, the filtered result can be checked by rerunning the decomposition with the same documented parameters and, where relevant, with alternative random seeds or nearby ensemble settings to confirm that retained scale bands and validation metrics are stable. [@Ref002][@Ref007] Visual review includes overlaid raw and filtered acceleration traces, removed-component traces, endpoint behavior, pre-event or post-event baseline segments when available, and plots of cumulative residuals or integrated histories so that the report shows whether the reconstruction preserves the features that control engineering response. [@Ref008][@Ref009]\n30\t\n31\t### SLOT 5: Description Without Overclaiming\n32\t\n33\tEEMD filtering is best described as an adaptive, data-driven filtering and reconstruction procedure applied to a measured vibration history. A conservative description states that selected IMFs were retained or removed according to documented engineering criteria, and that the resulting reconstruction was accepted only after checks on peak response, energy, frequency content, baseline behavior, and response spectra. [@Ref002][@Ref005][@Ref008] The description avoids stating that retained modes are physically unique blast-wave components unless separate evidence from instrumentation, site physics, wave-propagation modeling, or independent measurements supports that interpretation. [@Ref001][@Ref011]\n34\t\n35\tAppropriate terminology includes \"retained IMF group,\" \"removed scale band,\" \"EEMD-filtered reconstruction,\" \"adaptive denoising,\" and \"trend or residual removal,\" because those terms describe the processing operation without asserting a source mechanism. [@Ref003][@Ref006] Terms such as \"true blast component,\" \"physical mode,\" or \"isolated wave type\" require additional support and are not justified by the decomposition alone, because EMD separation depends on signal conditions and EEMD depends on noise-assisted ensemble choices. [@Ref011][@Ref002]\n36\t\n37\tA publication-ready limitation statement can say that EEMD was used to reduce unwanted scale content while preserving defined engineering measures, and that the filtered record is a processed representation of the measured history rather than a recovered unique physical waveform. [@Ref005][@Ref008] The report can further state that conclusions based on the filtered history are conditional on the documented EEMD parameters, component-selection rule, and validation results, and that raw and filtered histories remain distinguishable in the record archive and subsequent calculations. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n38\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9958eae5-5a95-4e8c-919f-b61a93167db8","uuid":"728b0a48-cb48-4e20-be11-b9036469da15","timestamp":"2026-07-06T20:44:05.576Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/averrik/Cloud/github/projects/AR-S2J2J.R/TITO/kb/T3.compile.research.md","content":"### SLOT 1: Authoritative EMD/EEMD Filtering References\n\nThe most authoritative starting point is Huang et al. (1998), because it introduced empirical mode decomposition as the decomposition stage of the Hilbert-Huang transform for nonlinear and non-stationary records and established the intrinsic mode function and residual reconstruction framework used by later filtering studies. This source supports EMD as an adaptive decomposition of a measured record, but it does not by itself justify treating any retained intrinsic mode function as a uniquely physical engineering component. [@Ref001] The central EEMD reference is Wu and Huang (2009), because it defines ensemble empirical mode decomposition as a noise-assisted method in which finite-amplitude white noise is added over repeated trials and ensemble averaging is used to reduce mode mixing and obtain a scale-organized decomposition. [@Ref002] Flandrin, Rilling, and Goncalves (2004) and Wu and Huang (2004) are also authoritative for filtering claims because they connect EMD behavior to filter-bank-like and noise-scale behavior, which is the main basis for describing IMFs as adaptive scale bands rather than preassigned physical modes. [@Ref003][@Ref004]\n\nFor engineering filtering rather than basic decomposition, Boudraa and Cexus (2007) and Kopsinis and McLaughlin (2009) are more directly relevant because they treat EMD as a signal filtering or denoising tool and address component selection or thresholding in measurement and signal-processing contexts. [@Ref005][@Ref006] Huang and Wu (2008) is a useful review source because it summarizes Hilbert-Huang transform applications in geophysical records and discusses open problems, making it appropriate for limitation language in an engineering report. [@Ref007] For transient acceleration histories, the EMD/EEMD sources are best paired with strong-motion processing references such as Boore and Bommer (2005), Boore (2005), and Boore and Akkar (2003), because those papers document the consequences of filtering, baseline correction, and filter choices on accelerograms and response spectra. [@Ref008][@Ref009][@Ref010]\n\n### SLOT 2: Supported Component Removal Practices\n\nThe literature supports component removal only as a documented filtering or detrending operation followed by reconstruction, not as automatic evidence that the removed or retained IMFs are physically unique blast-wave components. EMD/EEMD decompositions are adaptive and reconstructive, so a processed history can be formed by summing selected IMFs and, when appropriate, a residual term; however, the selection criterion has to be stated as an engineering or signal-processing rule, such as removal of components dominated by high-frequency noise, removal of a low-frequency trend, or retention of components inside a target response band. [@Ref001][@Ref002][@Ref003] A defensible report therefore presents IMF removal as scale-selective filtering based on evidence from the record, not as a decomposition into uniquely named source mechanisms. [@Ref007]\n\nRemoval of early, high-frequency IMFs is supported when those IMFs are shown to be dominated by measurement noise or by frequencies outside the engineering response range of interest. Boudraa and Cexus support EMD-based signal filtering, and Kopsinis and McLaughlin support denoising approaches that treat IMF content with thresholding logic rather than a blanket rule that the first IMF is always noise. [@Ref005][@Ref006] Removal of a residual or trend term can be defensible when the residual represents baseline drift or nonphysical low-frequency contamination, but the same operation can also remove real long-period motion; in acceleration-history applications it therefore has to be treated as a high-pass or trend-removal choice whose consequences are checked in integrated velocity, displacement, and response spectra. [@Ref008][@Ref009][@Ref010]\n\nSelection of nonadjacent or middle IMFs is also supported only when the selection is tied to a stated diagnostic, such as frequency content, energy contribution, correlation with the target signal, response-spectral relevance, or comparison with independent instrumentation or physics. Rilling and Flandrin show that EMD separation of nearby frequency content depends on signal conditions, so component selection cannot be assumed to isolate every true modal contribution in a short transient record. [@Ref011] EEMD reduces mode mixing by adding noise-assisted ensemble averaging, but the method still depends on ensemble size, added-noise amplitude, sifting choices, and endpoint treatment, so retained IMF groups are better described as selected scale bands than as verified physical modes. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n\n### SLOT 3: Known Limitations To Report\n\nMode mixing is a primary limitation to report. EEMD was proposed specifically to reduce the tendency of EMD to place disparate scales in the same IMF or split a similar scale across several IMFs, but its remedy uses added white noise and ensemble averaging rather than a deterministic physical separation. [@Ref002] This means that the reported processing has to identify the added-noise level, ensemble size, sifting or stopping criterion, randomization policy when relevant, and any post-processing or alignment method used to compare IMFs across trials. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n\nEnd effects, non-uniqueness, and parameter sensitivity are also material limitations. EMD envelopes are built from local extrema, so behavior near the beginning and end of a short record can be sensitive to interpolation and boundary extension choices; this is especially important for transient vibration histories in which the main pulse, decay tail, or baseline segment may occur near a boundary. [@Ref001][@Ref007] The decomposition is not a fixed orthogonal transform with a predeclared basis, and different sifting, stopping, noise, and endpoint choices can change the IMF set; therefore, the same measured history can yield different but plausible component partitions. [@Ref003][@Ref002]\n\nLoss of physical interpretability is a limitation whenever IMFs are used for report-level engineering claims. EMD and EEMD components are derived from local time-scale behavior, so the existence of an IMF group does not by itself demonstrate a separate blast source, wave path, soil layer response, sensor artifact, or structural mode. [@Ref001][@Ref011] Filtering can also alter peak values, apparent pulse duration, low-frequency drift, and response demands; those consequences are well documented in strong-motion filtering and baseline processing literature and are relevant when processed acceleration histories are used downstream. [@Ref008][@Ref010]\n\n### SLOT 4: Validation Checks For EEMD-Filtered Histories\n\nValidation of an EEMD-filtered acceleration history is strongest when it compares the raw record, the retained reconstruction, and the removed residual content in both time and frequency domains. Peak preservation checks include the signed and absolute peak acceleration, peak timing, pulse polarity, and local pulse shape near the peak, because a filter that improves visual smoothness can still alter the controlling demand in a transient history. [@Ref008][@Ref009] Baseline and drift checks include numerical integration to velocity and displacement, comparison of terminal velocity and displacement trends, and review of any low-frequency residual removed from the reconstruction. [@Ref008]\n\nEnergy and frequency-content checks are defensible because EMD filtering is scale-selective. The report can compare root-mean-square acceleration, summed squared acceleration, cumulative signal energy, Fourier or power spectral density estimates, Hilbert marginal spectra when used, and the frequency content of removed IMFs to confirm that the operation removed the intended scale range rather than suppressing engineering demand. [@Ref005][@Ref006][@Ref004] Response-spectrum checks are important when the processed acceleration is used as an engineering input, because response spectra can be sensitive to filtering choices and the difference may be consequential even when the acceleration trace appears acceptable. [@Ref010][@Ref008]\n\nRepeatability and visual diagnostics are also necessary for EEMD-filtered histories. Because EEMD depends on added-noise amplitude and ensemble averaging, the filtered result can be checked by rerunning the decomposition with the same documented parameters and, where relevant, with alternative random seeds or nearby ensemble settings to confirm that retained scale bands and validation metrics are stable. [@Ref002][@Ref007] Visual review includes overlaid raw and filtered acceleration traces, removed-component traces, endpoint behavior, pre-event or post-event baseline segments when available, and plots of cumulative residuals or integrated histories so that the report shows whether the reconstruction preserves the features that control engineering response. [@Ref008][@Ref009]\n\n### SLOT 5: Description Without Overclaiming\n\nEEMD filtering is best described as an adaptive, data-driven filtering and reconstruction procedure applied to a measured vibration history. A conservative description states that selected IMFs were retained or removed according to documented engineering criteria, and that the resulting reconstruction was accepted only after checks on peak response, energy, frequency content, baseline behavior, and response spectra. [@Ref002][@Ref005][@Ref008] The description avoids stating that retained modes are physically unique blast-wave components unless separate evidence from instrumentation, site physics, wave-propagation modeling, or independent measurements supports that interpretation. [@Ref001][@Ref011]\n\nAppropriate terminology includes \"retained IMF group,\" \"removed scale band,\" \"EEMD-filtered reconstruction,\" \"adaptive denoising,\" and \"trend or residual removal,\" because those terms describe the processing operation without asserting a source mechanism. [@Ref003][@Ref006] Terms such as \"true blast component,\" \"physical mode,\" or \"isolated wave type\" require additional support and are not justified by the decomposition alone, because EMD separation depends on signal conditions and EEMD depends on noise-assisted ensemble choices. [@Ref011][@Ref002]\n\nA publication-ready limitation statement can say that EEMD was used to reduce unwanted scale content while preserving defined engineering measures, and that the filtered record is a processed representation of the measured history rather than a recovered unique physical waveform. [@Ref005][@Ref008] The report can further state that conclusions based on the filtered history are conditional on the documented EEMD parameters, component-selection rule, and validation results, and that raw and filtered histories remain distinguishable in the record archive and subsequent calculations. [@Ref002][@Ref007]\n","numLines":38,"startLine":1,"totalLines":38}}}
