{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01NVofqFUBpbYCsXf6SfpJqV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the DOIs I need. Let me compile the full reference list:\n\n1. Huang et al. (1998) = [@Ref001] in T3 = [DOI:10.1098/rspa.1998.0193]\n2. Wu and Huang (2009) = [@Ref002] in T3 = [DOI:10.1142/S1793536909000047]\n3. Flandrin, Rilling, Goncalves (2004) = [@Ref003] in T3 = [DOI:10.1109/LSP.2003.821662]\n4. Wu and Huang (2004) = [@Ref004] in T3 = [DOI:10.1098/rspa.2003.1221]\n5. Boudraa and Cexus (2007) = [@Ref005] in T3 = [DOI:10.1109/TIM.2007.907967]\n6. Kopsinis and McLaughlin (2009) = [@Ref006] in T3 = [DOI:10.1109/TSP.2009.2013885]\n7. Huang and Wu (2008) = [@Ref007] in T\n\n3 = [DOI:10.1029/2007RG000228]\n8. Boore and Bommer (2005) = [@Ref008] in T3 = [DOI:10.1016/j.soildyn.2004.10.007]\n9. Boore (2005) = [@Ref009] in T3 = [DOI:10.1785/0120040160]\n10. Boore and Akkar (2003) = [@Ref010] in T3 = [DOI:10.1002/eqe.299]\n11. Rilling and Flandrin (2008) = [@Ref011] in T3 = [DOI:10.1109/TSP.2007.910389]\n\nI've compiled all the reference material I need, so I'm ready to start drafting the manuscript. on claim support, limitations, and validation expectations for EEMD as an engineering filtering method rather than basic definitions. I'll structure this around the five slots—authoritative references, supported removal practices, known limitations, validation checks, and careful claims—writing in flowing paragraphs while preserving the KB citations and adding DOI references, plus confidence footnotes for each prose section. I'm working through the formatting rules now—flowing paragraphs with no bullet points, academic tone, preserving the citation keys from the knowledge base passages, adding DOI references, and ending each paragraph with a confidence footnote. The slot headings should be formatted as level-three markdown headings to match the structured prompt specification.\n\nFor each slot, I'm planning to cover the foundational references first—starting with Huang et al. (1998) and Wu and Huang (2009) as the core EMD/EEMD sources, then moving into the supporting literature like Flandrin et al. (2004) and Wu and Huang (2004) on filtering approaches.\n\nSlot 2 will focus on the practical side of IMF removal—how to defensibly remove high-frequency noise, trend components, and select middle IMFs without claiming false physical uniqueness. Slot 3 tackles the known limitations: mode mixing, end effects, non-uniqueness, and the loss of interpretability. Slot 4 covers validation strategies like peak preservation, baseline checks, energy and frequency verification, and visual diagnostics. Slot 5 will explain how to frame EEMD filtering in a way that's honest about what it does and doesn't do. I need to keep the ASCII formatting rules in mind—using \"Goncalves\" instead of accented versions, straight quotes rather than smart quotes, and standard hyphens. Now I'm drafting the full manuscript with flowing paragraphs, preserved reference keys, audit footnotes after each section, and maintaining an academic tone throughout.\n\nHuang et al. (1998) introduced empirical mode decomposition as the foundational decomposition method within the Hilbert-Huang transform framework for analyzing nonlinear and non-stationary signals, establishing the intrinsic mode function and residual reconstruction approach that underpins all subsequent filtering applications. [KB:T3.compile.research.md][@Ref001][DOI:10.1098/rspa.1998.0193] While this seminal work demonstrates EMD's capacity to adaptively decompose measured records, it does not itself establish that any retained intrinsic mode function represents a uniquely physical engineering component—a limitation that remains central to interpreting filtering results.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: Huang et al. (1998) is the foundational EMD paper in Proceedings of the Royal Society A; the constraint on physical interpretability reflects standard scholarly reading and aligns with the broader literature on method limitations.] Wu and Huang (2009) then formalized ensemble empirical mode decomposition as a noise-assisted refinement that addresses mode mixing issues inherent in standard EMD. reference for EEMD, while Boudraa and Cexus (2007) and Kopsinis and McLaughlin (2009) are more applicable when treating EMD as a practical filtering or denoising approach with component selection strategies in signal processing contexts. of filtering, baseline correction, and filter choices on accelerograms and response spectra. Boore (2005) specifically addresses padding and filtering procedures for strong-motion data, particularly relevant to end-effect and integration behavior, while Boore and Akkar (2003) demonstrate how causal versus acausal filter choices significantly affect elastic and inelastic response spectra, with spectral consequences extending well below the filter corner frequency.\n\nFor component removal in EMD/EEMD decompositions, the literature supports this only as an explicit filtering or detrending operation with reconstruction, not as automatic identification of physically distinct blast-wave components. Since EMD and EEMD are adaptive and reconstructive, selected intrinsic mode functions can be summed to form a processed history, but the selection must follow a stated engineering or signal-processing criterion—such as removing high-frequency noise-dominated components or eliminating specific frequency bands.\n\nA defensible analysis therefore frames IMF removal as scale-selective filtering grounded in the actual record, rather than claiming the decomposition reveals uniquely named source mechanisms. Removing early high-frequency intrinsic mode functions is justified when those components are demonstrably dominated by measurement noise or frequencies outside the engineering response range of interest.\n\nRecent work by Boudraa and Cexus and by Kopsinis and McLaughlin supports EMD-based filtering and denoising with thresholding logic applied to individual IMFs, rather than treating the first IMF as automatically noise. Residual or trend removal can be defensible when addressing baseline drift or nonphysical low-frequency contamination, but the same operation risks removing real long-period motion; in acceleration applications, this choice must be validated by checking the consequences in integrated velocity, displacement, and response spectra. ance, or comparison with independent instrumentation or physics. Rilling and Flandrin's work demonstrates that EMD's ability to separate nearby frequencies depends heavily on signal conditions, so I can't assume component selection will capture every true modal contribution in short transient records. EEMD improves mode mixing through noise-assisted ensemble averaging, but the outcome still hinges on ensemble size, noise amplitude, sifting parameters, and boundary handling—meaning the retained mode function groups are better understood as selected scale bands rather than confirmed physical modes.\n\nThe high-confidence requirement for a stated diagnostic before selecting middle intrinsic mode functions aligns with this signal-condition dependence and the scale-band interpretation supported by recent literature. Mode mixing remains the primary limitation I need to report for any EEMD-filtered engineering history, since while EEMD was designed to address EMD's tendency to either combine disparate scales into one mode or split similar scales across multiple modes, it achieves this through added white noise and ensemble averaging rather than deterministic physical principles.\n\nWhen reporting EEMD processing, I must disclose the noise level added, ensemble size, sifting or stopping criteria, randomization approach where applicable, and any post-processing or alignment methods used to compare intrinsic mode functions across trials. End effects, non-uniqueness, and parameter sensitivity are also critical limitations to report. Since EMD envelopes depend on local extrema, behavior near record boundaries can be sensitive to interpolation and extension choices—particularly problematic for transient vibration data where the main pulse or decay might occur at a boundary. The decomposition isn't a fixed orthogonal transform with a predefined basis, so different sifting, stopping, noise, and endpoint choices can produce different but equally valid intrinsic mode function sets from the same measured signal, meaning results are conditional on the specific parameters chosen.\n\nThe non-uniqueness and end-effect limitations are well-documented in the literature, with established research on envelope interpolation, boundary behavior, and the data-driven nature of the decomposition. Loss of physical interpretability becomes a concern when using intrinsic mode functions for engineering claims, since EMD and EEMD components reflect local time-scale behavior rather than inherent physical properties. of inherent physical meaning in the intrinsic mode functions is well supported by foundational work from Huang and colleagues as well as Rilling and Flandrin, with downstream effects on peak values and response spectra documented across multiple studies. Now I'm moving into validation approaches for EEMD-filtered records, which requires comparing the raw acceleration history against the reconstructed signal and removed residual content across both time and frequency domains—paying particular attention to whether filtering preserves the signed and absolute peak acceleration, peak timing, pulse polarity, and local pulse shape, since smoothing can mask alterations to the controlling demand in transient behavior.\n\nBeyond peak preservation, I'm also checking baseline stability and drift through numerical integration to velocity and displacement, examining terminal velocity and displacement trends, and reviewing any low-frequency content that was removed during reconstruction.\n\nEnergy and frequency-content checks validate that EMD filtering is scale-selective by comparing root-mean-square acceleration, cumulative signal energy, spectral density estimates, and the frequency content of removed components to confirm the operation targeted the intended scale range without suppressing engineering-relevant signals. Response-spectrum checks become critical when the processed acceleration serves as engineering input, since response spectra can be quite sensitive to filtering choices. the filtered result can be validated by rerunning the decomposition with the same parameters and alternative random seeds to confirm stability across ensemble settings. Visual diagnostics should include overlaid raw and filtered traces, removed components, endpoint behavior, and cumulative residual plots to verify that the reconstruction preserves the features governing engineering response.\n\nFor the engineering report, I'm describing EEMD as an adaptive, data-driven filtering procedure where selected intrinsic mode functions were retained or removed based on documented criteria, with the final reconstruction validated through checks on peak response, energy, frequency content, baseline behavior, and response spectra—avoiding overclaims about the method's capabilities. When reporting results, I need to use terminology that describes the processing operation itself—like \"retained intrinsic mode function group,\" \"removed scale band,\" \"EEMD-filtered reconstruction,\" or \"adaptive denoising\"—rather than claiming to have isolated specific physical phenomena. Terms implying direct physical identification like \"true blast component\" or \"isolated wave type\" aren't justified by decomposition alone, since the separation depends on signal conditions and the ensemble noise choices in EEMD.\n\nFor the publication, I can frame the limitation by stating that EEMD was applied to reduce unwanted scale content while preserving the engineering measures of interest, and clarify that the filtered record is a processed representation of the measured history rather than a recovered unique physical waveform. I'm checking the audit requirements against my draft: verifying that all citations use proper token form with citekeys, confirming ASCII-only characters (I used \"Goncalves\" without the cedilla to stay within ASCII), and ensuring the DOI citation hierarchy is followed correctly. I need to preserve both the [@Ref] citekeys from the KB passages and the [DOI:...] tokens I'm adding, which my draft handles correctly. For the document structure, I'm considering whether to add an H1 title at the top—while the reference example starts directly with slot headings, a title would be more professional for this research output. I'm checking my draft against the formatting requirements—making sure there are no accented characters in the body text, that paragraphs are properly separated by blank lines, and that each prose block ends with its audit footnote before the blank line. I should also scan the text for any problematic language patterns that need converting to more academic phrasing, like replacing \"makes\" or \"enables\" with more formal alternatives.\n\nLooking at my paragraph distribution across the five slots, I have 16 paragraphs total with corresponding audit footnotes, which maintains consistency throughout the document.\n\nBut I'm noticing a structural issue in SLOT 1 - I've separated the Huang et al. (1998) introduction from the Wu and Huang (2009) and Flandrin references into different paragraphs, when in the original template they're all part of one continuous paragraph. I need to reconsider whether I should consolidate these back together to match the source structure more closely. I'm seeing the rest of the references for engineering applications and strong-motion work, so I'll reorganize into three focused paragraphs instead of two larger ones. Each paragraph will handle a distinct conceptual area—foundational work, EEMD methodology, and engineering-specific filtering—with one audit footnote per paragraph to keep things granular.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2167,"cache_read_input_tokens":68608,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2167,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":39,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9958eae5-5a95-4e8c-919f-b61a93167db8","uuid":"9566ae03-bb6c-4def-a4ce-4e4d96fe02ea","request_id":"req_011CcmTY4JhcEBxECRyL5a2a"}
